    "created_at": "2017-08-17T11:12:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-08-17T11:12:26Z",
    "body": "Well, I can see that implementations might want to not make unnecessary copies but also not be forced to keep references to an unlimited quantity of objects around forever.  Especially for something like getElementsByName(), you could imagine some code might call it thousands of times in a loop with the same value (perhaps for a microbenchmark), so you don't want to make a new copy each time.  While other code might call it thousands of times in a loop with different values, so you don't want to keep them all around.\r\n\r\nIt's probably easier to optimize returning a new copy each time, because copies can be made reasonably cheap if they share the underlying list.  But it's probably not as nice for authors to have lots of non-equal but completely interchangeable objects floating around.\r\n\r\nWhat's WebKit \"and co\"?  Just WebKit, or other UAs also?  At the end of the day, we could leave this undefined if UAs really don't want to be constrained.  It wouldn't be the end of the world in terms of interop.  But always better to have it specced.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
